<Docs slug="sandbox">
  <h1>Sandbox</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Run an agent with its permission checks off without handing it your machine. Ouijit's sandbox limits a
    command to its task's worktree, and you choose what runs inside it: a whole terminal, a hook, or one command.
  </p>

  <h2>Where things run</h2>
  <p>
    Each project has a <strong>Default</strong> under <strong>Project Settings &rarr; Sandbox</strong>: Host,
    Sandbox, or Custom. Task terminals, hooks, Run, and scripts all run there unless something more specific
    says otherwise:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One terminal.</strong> Right-click a task and use <strong>Open in</strong>. It lists a Terminal for each place, the default first.</li>
    <li><strong>One run of a hook.</strong> The dialog that appears when you move a task starts on the hook's setting and can change it for that run.</li>
    <li><strong>One Run or script.</strong> <strong>Run in</strong> at the top of a terminal's <strong>+</strong> menu.</li>
    <li><strong>A hook, every time.</strong> Each of start, continue, review, and done has its own setting, which starts on Default. Set it when a hook must always run somewhere else, such as a done hook that pushes with credentials only the host has.</li>
    <li><strong>Part of a command.</strong> <code>ouijit sandbox -- &lt;command&gt;</code> runs just that command in the sandbox, from a host terminal. See <a href="#command">below</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A sandboxed terminal carries an outlined status dot and a suffix on its label, and so does a host terminal
    while a command started with <code>ouijit sandbox</code> is running. A sandboxed runner's tab dot is
    outlined too.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the chosen sandbox cannot start, the terminal says why and Ouijit raises an error. It never runs the
    command on the host instead, and a restored terminal whose sandbox is gone fails the same way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="command">Sandboxing one command</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ouijit sandbox</code> runs a single command in the sandbox with the grants its terminal's task would
    get, while the rest of the line stays on the host. A start hook can pull on the host and contain only the
    agent:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git pull origin main &amp;&amp; ouijit sandbox -- claude --dangerously-skip-permissions "$OUIJIT_TASK_NAME"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Add <code>--custom</code> to use the Custom sandbox. The command gets an API token of its own at the
    project's <a href="#cli">CLI access</a> level, never the host terminal's, and loses it when it exits. A
    host step that runs after a sandboxed one can still execute files the agent changed, such as tests it
    edited, so the sandbox protects your machine while the agent works rather than from what it leaves behind.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cli">The Ouijit CLI inside a sandbox</h2>
  <p>Under <strong>Project Settings &rarr; Sandbox &rarr; Ouijit CLI access</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read only</strong>, the default. A sandboxed command can read its own task, its diff notes and pull requests, draft pull request and Linear comments, point you at things, and ask you to run a command. On its own terminal it can read the screen, add tags, read panels, and open markdown files from its worktree.</li>
    <li><strong>Its own task.</strong> It can also move, rename and describe its own task, link it to a pull request or issue, and add to-do tasks. It can read every terminal on its task. Moving a task runs that column's hook as usual, but a hook that would run on the host waits for you to open it, even with <code>--run-hook</code>; a one-off <code>--hook-command</code> or <code>--sandbox</code> is refused.</li>
    <li><strong>Everything.</strong> It can do anything a host terminal can, which includes having Ouijit run commands on the host. Choose this only for agents you would run unsandboxed anyway.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ouijit's sandbox</h2>
  <p>
    Ouijit's sandbox is <a href="https://github.com/nolabs-ai/nono" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nono</a>,
    which runs a command in place on the host worktree under kernel-level access limits (Seatbelt on macOS,
    Landlock on Linux). It is a kernel access policy on a host process rather than a separate machine, so it
    starts instantly with no file sync.
  </p>
  <h3>What it grants</h3>
  <p>
    Filesystem access is limited to the worktree and the repository's git data: <code>.git</code> is read-only
    apart from read/write overlays at <code>objects/</code>, <code>refs/</code>, <code>logs/</code>, and
    <code>worktrees/</code>, so commits land while <code>hooks/</code> and <code>config</code> stay read-only.
    Ouijit also grants a per-project package cache and keeps its status port reachable, so agent status
    reports back even when the network is blocked. These are derived from the task at spawn time.
  </p>
  <h3>The profile</h3>
  <p>
    Everything else is a nono profile file. Each project gets its own, at
    <code>~/.config/nono/profiles/ouijit-&lt;project&gt;-&lt;id&gt;.json</code>, written once to extend Ouijit's
    base profile (which lets Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, and Pi sign in and run) and then left to you. Open
    it from <strong>Sandbox profile</strong> in settings to block the network, open ports, or grant folders,
    in nono's own <a href="https://nono.sh/docs/cli/features/profile-authoring" target="_blank" rel="noopener">profile format</a>.
    <strong>Change&hellip;</strong> points the project at another profile file, such as one your team shares,
    and <strong>Reset</strong> goes back to the project's own. Profiles live outside the repository, where a
    sandboxed agent cannot edit its own permissions.
  </p>

  <h3>Requirements</h3>
  <p>Ouijit bundles the <code>nono</code> binary and the agent packs it uses; there is nothing extra to install.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS</strong>. Seatbelt (<code>sandbox-exec</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Linux</strong>. Landlock, which needs kernel 5.13 or newer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="custom">Custom</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Read this first.</strong> A plain task terminal carries a host-scoped <code>OUIJIT_API_TOKEN</code>.
    A launcher you run by hand inside one passes that token into the sandbox, where an agent can rewrite hooks
    and this very setting, so the next spawn runs its command on the host. Configure the launcher here and use
    <code>ouijit sandbox --custom -- &lt;command&gt;</code>, which gives the command a sandbox-scoped token of
    its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Custom backend runs a task's terminals under a launcher you provide: a reviewed script in your team's
    control, with its own pinned sandbox binary, committed policy, and preflight checks. Ouijit grants nothing
    itself; the launcher owns the boundary. Set the command under
    <strong>Project Settings &rarr; Sandbox &rarr; Custom launcher</strong> or with <code>ouijit sandbox-command set</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>What Ouijit passes</h3>
  <p>
    Ouijit runs <code>&lt;launcher&gt; [args] -- &lt;shell&gt; [shell args]</code> with the working directory set
    to the task worktree and the full terminal environment: <code>OUIJIT_API_URL</code> and a
    <strong>sandbox-scoped</strong> <code>OUIJIT_API_TOKEN</code>, <code>OUIJIT_PTY_ID</code>,
    <code>OUIJIT_WRAPPER_DIR</code>, <code>OUIJIT_CLI_PATH</code> (when the bundled CLI is present),
    <code>OUIJIT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR</code>,
    and the shell integration's <code>ZDOTDIR</code>. The launcher's argv is exactly the configured command,
    split on whitespace with shell-style quoting. Nothing is expanded except a leading <code>~/</code> on the
    launcher path, and nothing is added except the <code>--</code> and the shell.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything Ouijit would otherwise have granted is exported as advisory hints. They are computed on the host
    from the project path and Ouijit's own directories, never from the worktree, and a launcher is free to ignore
    them.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>What Ouijit's other backends do with it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_WORKTREE</code></td><td>The task worktree, read+write.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_GIT_DIR</code></td><td>The repository's <code>.git</code>, read-only.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_GIT_WRITABLE_DIRS</code></td><td>Colon-separated <code>objects</code>, <code>refs</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>worktrees</code> under it, writable so commits land while <code>hooks/</code> and <code>config</code> stay read-only.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_HOOK_PORT</code></td><td>The localhost port that must stay reachable for agent status to report back.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_CACHE_DIR</code></td><td>A per-project package-manager cache outside any worktree, read+write. Shared by every task of the project.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_WRAPPER_DIR</code></td><td>Ouijit's agent shims and CLI reference, read-only. Its <code>bin/</code> is first on <code>PATH</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OUIJIT_SANDBOX_CLI_DIR</code></td><td>The bundled <code>ouijit</code> CLI the shim runs, read-only. Set only when the bundled CLI is present.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>What the launcher is responsible for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Exec the argv after <code>--</code> with the inherited environment intact: at least <code>PATH</code>, <code>ZDOTDIR</code>, every <code>OUIJIT_*</code> variable, <code>TERM</code>, <code>HOME</code>, <code>SHELL</code>, and the locale. Shell integration, the status dot, and the <code>ouijit</code> and agent shims are all carried by the environment; a launcher that scrubs it starts a bare shell.</li>
    <li>Own the boundary. Ouijit adds no <code>--allow</code> or <code>--read</code> flags.</li>
    <li>Keep the hook port reachable if you block the network.</li>
    <li>Exit non-zero to refuse. Whatever it wrote to stderr appears in the terminal, the exit line prints red, and Ouijit raises a "sandbox failed to start" notice. It never falls back to an unsandboxed shell.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Where the launcher may live</h3>
  <p>
    An absolute path or a bare name resolved on <code>PATH</code>. Relative paths are refused, and so is any
    absolute path inside the worktree or the spawn directory: the sandboxed agent can write there, and a
    launcher it can edit is one it can turn off. Keep a versioned launcher in the repo if you like, but install
    it from a reviewed checkout to somewhere the sandbox cannot write, such as <code>~/.local/bin</code>, and
    point the setting there. If your <code>PATH</code> contains relative entries, use an absolute path.
  </p>
</Docs>
